{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'primitive.*function argument|primitive.*first-class|primitive.*closure|host function|:yin.k/primitive|primitive.*value' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | head -70\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '650,725p'\"","aggregated_output":"docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:298:  name, or a call of a primitive that receives a function argument,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:897:time with host functions. This is the \"static ad-hoc in-memory\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:900:code (host functions) rather than naming it.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1290:  which only a host function can fabricate, resolves through the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1627:  maps each assumed host function to its profile address (UCF section\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1800:above is a declaration check, not a proof.** A host function is opaque\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1815:  that no host function ever arrives by linking. It makes no claim\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1819:  `docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`: an effectful host function\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:11:2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:44:host functions, and nothing says which primitives it assumed.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:362:`:call` — a called host function that returns an effect blocks exactly as an\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:549:The first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:552:so a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:565:| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:597:§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:598:primitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:615::yin.k/primitives {+\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:631:- **A host function under no name is refused** (kind `:unnamed-function`) —\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:752: :yin.k/primitives      {sym → profile, §7.5.2}                        ; every {:yin.k/primitive …} encoded, plus every free :var\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:774:  contributes a stream identity; every `:yin.k/primitive` marker contributes\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:903:                   :yin.k/primitives     {println <expected profile>}\n   650\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n   651\t\n   652\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\n   653\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\n   654\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\n   655\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\n   656\t\n   657\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n   658\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\n   659\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\n   660\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\n   661\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n   662\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n   663\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n   664\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n   665\t  for correctness of sharing.\n   666\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n   667\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\n   668\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\n   669\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\n   670\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\n   671\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\n   672\t  draft's two competing ones.\n   673\t\n   674\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\n   675\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\n   676\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n   677\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\n   678\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\n   679\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n   680\t\n   681\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n   682\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n   683\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n   684\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n   685\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n   686\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n   687\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\n   688\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\n   689\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n   690\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n   691\t\n   692\t```clojure\n   693\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n   694\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n   695\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\n   696\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n   697\t```\n   698\t\n   699\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\n   700\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\n   701\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\n   702\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\n   703\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n   704\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\n   705\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n   706\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n   707\t\n   708\tA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\n   709\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\n   710\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\n   711\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\n   712\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\n   713\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\n   714\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\n   715\tobject.\n   716\t\n   717\t### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\n   718\t\n   719\tEncoding a continuation with one refused leaf refuses the whole\n   720\tcontinuation:\n   721\t\n   722\t```clojure\n   723\t{:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\n   724\t :yin.k/path   [:yin.k/env 'conn]         ; where in the frame\n   725\t :yin.k/kind   :host-object               ; the closed kind set below\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
