{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '591,616p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '293,311p;419,425p;451,465p'\nnl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc | sed -n '401,425p;469,479p'\"","aggregated_output":"   591\tcannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\n   592\trejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\n   593\tMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\n   594\t\n   595\t### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\n   596\t\n   597\t§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\n   598\tprimitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\n   599\t(`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter recognizes such a value by reverse\n   600\tlookup in its own `:primitives` map — identity on the function object. The\n   601\tfirst draft treated the recovered symbol as the whole story. It is not:\n   602\t\n   603\t- one function object may sit under **several names**, making reverse lookup\n   604\t  ambiguous;\n   605\t- two composition-supplied primitive maps may bind the **same symbol to\n   606\t  different functions** — name presence at the resumer proves nothing about\n   607\t  behavior;\n   608\t- identity stability says nothing about **purity or captured host state**.\n   609\t\n   610\tUCF therefore binds every portable function to a **primitive profile**,\n   611\tpublished data, and the binding is checked on both ends:\n   612\t\n   613\t```clojure\n   614\t;; in :yin.k/requires — computed, not hand-written (§7.6.1)\n   615\t:yin.k/primitives {+\n   616\t                   {:yin.k/profile :yin.k.pp/sha256-…   ; content address of the profile record\n   293\t  `:conditional?` is true when any `:if` branch, jump target range, or\n   294\t  lambda body encloses the definition; `:in-body?` is true when a\n   295\t  lambda body encloses the occurrence; an `:applications-fn` record is\n   296\t  one **application site**: a position at which this image may apply a\n   297\t  closure it creates -- a direct call site, a call of an imported\n   298\t  name, or a call of a primitive that receives a function argument,\n   299\t  because the callee may apply what it is handed. Dominance between\n   300\t  two positions is decided by the linker from these fields and the\n   301\t  format's order (step 5a). For the AST record all three scanners are\n   302\t  Datalog over the rows: `yin.vm/free-names` today returns a symbol\n   303\t  set, and is extended (or wrapped by an occurrence-scoped query over\n   304\t  the same rules) to return each occurrence's path; the definitions\n   305\t  scanner is the store-key query behind `yin.vm/ast-requirements`\n   306\t  with the binding row's path and its enclosing tags; the application\n   307\t  scanner is the application-row query over the same relation. For\n   308\t  the vector formats all three are operand scans by pc (`:var`\n   309\t  against `:store-put`, `:load-free` against the `yin/def` call\n   310\t  sites, the call opcodes for applications) with conditional target\n   311\t  ranges taken from the `:jump-if` and body operands. A format whose\n   419\t     A definition discharges an occurrence by **defined-before-use\n   420\t     under control-flow dominance**, not by subtraction of names and\n   421\t     not by source order alone. Source order says a definition is\n   422\t     written earlier; dominance says it has executed by the time the\n   423\t     occurrence runs. Only the second discharges, and where dominance\n   424\t     cannot be established the obligation is **retained**, never\n   425\t     dropped:\n   451\t     - An occurrence inside a lambda body is discharged only by an\n   452\t       unconditional definition of that name that is **proven to\n   453\t       execute before every application of the enclosing closure**.\n   454\t       The body runs at application time, and the applications the\n   455\t       image performs are exactly its application sites\n   456\t       (`:applications-fn`, section 4.1). No value-flow analysis\n   457\t       bounds which closures a site can apply, so every site is\n   458\t       treated as a possible application of every closure the image\n   459\t       creates: a definition discharges a body occurrence only when\n   460\t       it dominates every application site in the main sequence,\n   461\t       under the same order rules as a main-sequence occurrence. A\n   462\t       site inside another body needs no separate case: it executes\n   463\t       only after some main-sequence site has executed, which the\n   464\t       definition dominates, or after halt, by which an unconditional\n   465\t       definition has executed. An image with no application site\n   401\t(def ^:private tree-yin-def-application-query\n   402\t  \"Every application of the `yin/def` variable, with its operand ids\n   403\t   (section 4.1): the sites whose key the definition query may not have\n   404\t   read as a constant.\"\n   405\t  '[:find ?path ?operands\n   406\t    :in $ $occ ?root\n   407\t    :where\n   408\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   409\t    [?v :application ?op ?operands _]\n   410\t    [?op :variable yin/def]])\n   411\t\n   412\t\n   413\t(defn- computed-yin-def-write?\n   414\t  \"Whether the module has a `yin/def` application whose key is not\n   415\t   statically a constant: any shape but two operands, the first a\n   416\t   literal, may write a name that evaluates to `yin/def`.\"\n   417\t  [{:keys [rows]} db occ root]\n   418\t  (boolean\n   419\t    (some (fn [[_ operands]]\n   420\t            (not (and (= 2 (count operands))\n   421\t                      (= :literal\n   422\t                         (nth (get rows (nth operands 0)) 1)))))\n   423\t          (query/collect\n   424\t            (query/q tree-yin-def-application-query db occ root)))))\n   425\t\n   469\t                           {:name key, :at [root at-path],\n   470\t                            :yin-def? yin-def?,\n   471\t                            :conditional? conditional?})))\n   472\t                  (sort-by (comp second :at) path-order)\n   473\t                  vec)]\n   474\t    (->> (if (or (some (fn [d] (= 'yin/def (:name d))) defs)\n   475\t                 (computed-yin-def-write? tree db occ root))\n   476\t           (filterv (complement :yin-def?) defs)\n   477\t           defs)\n   478\t         (mapv (fn [d] (dissoc d :yin-def?))))))\n   479\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 11:53:55 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 18:53:55 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nThe direct `:vm/store-put` and computed-key cases are now guarded, and the earlier invocation-order and bounds fixes remain intact. One alias path still blocks sign-off.\n\nP1 | [linker.cljc:401](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:401) | The guard recognizes calls whose operator is visibly `yin/def`, but primitives are first-class values ([UCF:597](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:597)). A lambda can receive `yin/def` as `setter` and call `(setter 'yin/def 0)` before `(yin/def 'x 1)`. That first call writes the active store, yet matches neither the direct-key check nor the computed-key query; the scanner then incorrectly discharges a subsequent read of `x` ([linker.cljc:474](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:474)). | Account for effectful primitive aliases when proving `yin/def` unshadowed, retaining obligations wherever a prior call may write its slot. Add a nested alias-call regression test.\n\nThe AST `:vm/store-put` key is a direct `:key` slot, not an evaluated child ([yin.vm.cljc:807](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:807)). The independent tri-host runs and style check are green; clj-kondo was not available. They do not cover the alias case.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\nSign-off: DENIED"}}
